---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { story, testimonials } from '../data/site.js';

// Prose is plain text between <p> tags — edit it like a document.
// The quotes come from src/data/site.js.
---

<BaseLayout title="Our Story" description="How Helping Hands began and why it still runs.">
  <img class="banner" src={story.bannerImage} alt={story.bannerAlt} />

  <div class="split">
    <div class="split-text">
      <h1>Our Story</h1>

      <p>
        Kushal and Anurag&rsquo;s inspiration to start Helping Hands came in the
        summer of 2020, when the coronavirus pandemic changed the lives of
        students all over the world. Realizing that students would not have
        access to traditional summer schooling and camps, Kushal and Anurag
        realized that kids would not have much to do over the summer.
      </p>

      <p>
        We decided to start Helping Hands, an organization that
        offers free math and science classes, conducted online for middle and
        high school students across the United States. We hope to serve all
        students and grow as an organization, helping people who need it the
        most.
      </p>
    </div>

    <aside>
      <h2 class="quotes-heading">What people say</h2>

      <div class="quotes">
        {testimonials.map((t) => (
          <figure class="quote">
            <blockquote><p>{t.quote}</p></blockquote>
            <figcaption>
              {t.attribution}{t.role && <span class="quote-role">{t.role}</span>}
            </figcaption>
          </figure>
        ))}
      </div>
    </aside>
  </div>

  <h2>Why we keep teaching</h2>

  <div class="split">
    <div class="split-text">
      <!-- DRAFT — this is your motivation, so put it in your own words. -->
      <p>
        Teaching a concept forces you to understand it properly. You cannot hide
        behind a formula you half-remember when a twelve-year-old asks why it
        works. Every class makes us better at the subject, and every student who
        says the thing finally made sense is a reason to schedule the next one.
      </p>

      <p>
        The best sessions are the ones that go sideways. A student asks
        something we have not thought about since we learned it ourselves, and
        we have to work it out on the spot, in front of them. They see that
        figuring something out is normal rather than something only teachers can
        do. That is usually the moment a class stops being a lecture.
      </p>

      <p>
        Some students come back for a subject they have already covered at
        school. They are not there for the grade, because there is no grade.
        They come because it made sense the last time and they want that again.
        Six years in, that is still the clearest signal we have that this is
        worth running.
      </p>

      <p>
        None of this is a favour we are doing. We get more out of it than we put
        in.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="split-media">
      <img src={story.teachingImage} alt={story.teachingAlt} />
    </div>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>